WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:03.436
-Welcome back, welcome back.
"Batwoman."

00:00:03.436 --> 00:00:04.770
-Yeah.
-It's official.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:06.072
-Oh, it's very official.

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:08.608
It comes out -- Last time,
we were announcing it,

00:00:08.608 --> 00:00:10.076
and now it's airing.

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:11.878
But I feel like I saw you
like a week ago.

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:13.013
-I want to hear about this.

00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:14.880
I want to know --
what did it feel like

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.150
the first time
you put on the suit?

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.884
Talk to me.
-The suit felt amazing.

00:00:18.884 --> 00:00:21.621
Colleen Atwood designed it.
She is an Oscar winner.

00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:24.424
It's like a second skin
and it feels like

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:27.127
you're faster and stronger
and better and bulletproof,

00:00:27.127 --> 00:00:28.327
but you're none of those things.

00:00:28.327 --> 00:00:30.130
You're a little slower.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:32.731
And it's a little uncomfortable
on the back after a while.

00:00:32.731 --> 00:00:34.734
-But, I mean, I see there's ads
all over the place right now.

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:36.369
They're doing a great job
marketing this thing.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:38.137
But you look so cool.
-It's everywhere.

00:00:38.137 --> 00:00:39.471
-You look great.

00:00:39.471 --> 00:00:42.074
-Like, I've been living in
a bubble that is a Batcave

00:00:42.074 --> 00:00:44.577
that is -- It's in Vancouver.
which is the city

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:46.713
I don't even live in,
like, until now.

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:48.748
And then I come here and I,
like, had a panic attack

00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:50.416
because I was like,
"Oh, my God, this is amazing.

00:00:50.416 --> 00:00:51.717
I'm there and I'm there."

00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:54.086
And then a bus, like,
of myself swiped to my face,

00:00:54.086 --> 00:00:55.255
and I was like, "Whoa!

00:00:55.255 --> 00:00:56.655
Well, that would have been
really ironic."

00:00:56.655 --> 00:00:59.358
And then there was, like --
And then I saw it on, like,

00:00:59.358 --> 00:01:00.793
a little subway thing
and I was like,

00:01:00.793 --> 00:01:01.927
"I need to get back
to my hotel.

00:01:01.927 --> 00:01:03.462
This is just --"
It really freaked me out.

00:01:03.462 --> 00:01:04.597
-This is the first time --

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:06.266
No one has ever played
Batwoman before.

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:08.801
-No one has ever played her,
except me now.

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:10.903
And it comes out next week,
and I'm terrified.

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:12.905
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I'm so happy for you.

00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:14.540
-I'm happy. I'm thrilled.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.644
-And can we talk about something
that had happened on set?

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:20.246
You had an injury.
-I did.

00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:21.747
-Explain this.
This is, like --

00:01:21.747 --> 00:01:24.016
-Well, I had a couple of,
like, comedies of error

00:01:24.016 --> 00:01:26.452
when you're really hyped
about a show.

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:29.221
One was that I was allergic
to my cowl, my mask.

00:01:29.221 --> 00:01:31.424
And, so, I'm, like,
allergic to the adhesive tape,

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:34.093
so I had, like, hives for,
like, a little while.

00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:35.295
-You're joking.
-No.

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:38.064
About five days, six days in,
it was like,

00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:40.700
we took -- On, like,
the morning I was like

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.635
a pre-Proactiv
15-year-old-child

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:43.636
kind of commercial.

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:45.137
-Yeah.

00:01:45.137 --> 00:01:49.041
-And then, when I took it off,
I was, like, in a horror film,

00:01:49.041 --> 00:01:51.677
like, where people were like,
"It's going to be okay.

00:01:51.677 --> 00:01:54.246
Can you just stay back there?
Just stay there."

00:01:54.246 --> 00:01:55.848
-"Who are you?"
-"We're going to call someone.

00:01:55.848 --> 00:01:57.450
Just don't take a step.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.751
No, don't come near me.
Don't."

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:01.187
-So, what did they have to do?
Change the whole make --

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:04.390
-Yeah, we had to take everything
out of it and put, like,

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:05.824
something else --
cotton or something -- in it.

00:02:05.824 --> 00:02:08.360
And then -- But before that, I
broke my neck, basically,

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:09.462
on the show, as well.

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:10.830
-Talk to me about this,
'cause this is --

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.465
You can't even just say
that flippantly.

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:13.899
It's awful.

00:02:13.899 --> 00:02:16.235
-Well, that one -- You know,
that's, like, more funny.

00:02:16.235 --> 00:02:17.370
No, it's not at all.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:20.172
So, I did a stunt,
and for, like,

00:02:20.172 --> 00:02:22.741
a very extended amount of
time -- like seven hours --

00:02:22.741 --> 00:02:25.611
and we thought that I had broke
a rib or just, like,

00:02:25.611 --> 00:02:26.880
fractured a rib,
and that was like

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.215
6 to 12 weeks of, like, healing.

00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:32.518
So, then I had 6 to 12 weeks
of chronic pain

00:02:32.518 --> 00:02:35.855
and just sort of kept assuming
that that what it was,

00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:37.990
until I kept seeing
these doctors, and they're like,

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.124
"It's your neck,
it's your neck."

00:02:39.124 --> 00:02:41.026
It just, like,
radiates into these places

00:02:41.026 --> 00:02:42.061
because of nerve endings."

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:43.695
So I finally got an MRI.

00:02:43.695 --> 00:02:46.565
And I had to get it in Romania,
in the middle of a film,

00:02:46.565 --> 00:02:48.567
where I was also doing stunts.

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:50.103
And I had to send it
to my doctor,

00:02:50.103 --> 00:02:51.571
who had to sort of transcribe
the whole thing.

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:52.838
And, basically,
he called and he was like,

00:02:52.838 --> 00:02:54.540
"You could become paraplegic.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.108
This is your spine.

00:02:56.108 --> 00:02:58.711
Two of your disks
have herniated,

00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:01.046
and they've broken all
the protective layers.

00:03:01.046 --> 00:03:03.482
You have, like, this tiny amount
that your spine is, like,

00:03:03.482 --> 00:03:06.018
not severed,
and if you don't get back,

00:03:06.018 --> 00:03:07.419
you could become paraplegic."

00:03:07.419 --> 00:03:09.722
Even just, like, sleeping wrong
or, you know,

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:11.223
moving my head
in a strange direction.

00:03:11.223 --> 00:03:12.825
-Ruby, this is awful.

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:14.727
-It was so -- It was really,
really terrifying.

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:17.530
And then, you know, I was like,

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:18.864
"Well, at least
I don't have an option."

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:20.399
You know what I mean?
It's like, "I have to do it."

00:03:20.399 --> 00:03:21.700
-Yeah.
-So I did the surgery.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:23.269
And it was --
And, also, I couldn't feel

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:26.105
my arms for a long time,
but you just get used to

00:03:26.105 --> 00:03:27.540
weird things like that.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.043
And, so, it was only when
I would, like,

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:31.544
pick things up
and then I would drop them,

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:34.647
and then I was like,
"Well, that's strange."

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:37.516
And then I really didn't --

00:03:37.516 --> 00:03:38.717
I wasn't paying attention
to my body.

00:03:38.717 --> 00:03:40.052
I wasn't listening.

00:03:40.052 --> 00:03:41.387
I was, like, in such a hurry to
get everything done.

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:42.721
It was like, "'Batwoman.'
Yeah, I just did the pilot.

00:03:42.721 --> 00:03:44.190
And now I'm going to do
this film.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:45.591
I'm going to come back, we're
going to pick up the series."

00:03:45.591 --> 00:03:47.626
But I just wasn't listening,
and I think that was

00:03:47.626 --> 00:03:49.829
the world's way of saying like,
"You're going to have to

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:51.564
pay attention and you're gonna
have to slow down

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:53.432
and you're not actually
a superhero

00:03:53.432 --> 00:03:55.368
and you need to get
some priorities straight."

00:03:55.368 --> 00:03:56.836
-Wow.

00:03:56.836 --> 00:03:59.472
-And I went back to work after
10 days, because, like I said,

00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:03.375
my priorities were straight
and I liked to rest.

00:04:03.375 --> 00:04:04.510
-Did you freak out?

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.913
Did you just go,
"Well, what's --" I mean...

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:08.380
-Well, I didn't have time
to freak out.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.717
I was just like, "This happens
to like a lot of people."

00:04:11.717 --> 00:04:13.886
I mean, it got me thinking about
a lot of things,

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:15.989
Just like, for instance,
how do people pay

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:17.289
for the surgery if they need it?

00:04:17.289 --> 00:04:20.059
What happens if someone has
the same diagnosis as me,

00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:23.128
but they don't have insurance
and they can't get the surgery?

00:04:23.128 --> 00:04:24.330
It's not a cheap surgery.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.298
And they have to just,
like, live

00:04:26.298 --> 00:04:28.601
not knowing if they could
become paraplegic?

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:30.669
It just --
It was kind of terrifying.

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:33.105
And then my insurance
didn't cover it,

00:04:33.105 --> 00:04:35.040
and I was like, "Wait a minute.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.576
This is America."

00:04:37.576 --> 00:04:40.212
And, so -- But at least I have
the funds to pay it.

00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:41.648
You know what I mean?

00:04:41.648 --> 00:04:43.082
It's just, like -- It got me
thinking about so many things.

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:44.149
And then --

00:04:44.149 --> 00:04:45.150
-You appreciate everything,
right?

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:46.285
-I appreciate everything.

00:04:46.285 --> 00:04:48.354
And then I was really depressed,

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:50.623
because I wasn't allowed
to move for 10 days,

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:52.625
except to use the restroom.

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:56.562
And I got so depressed there
just thinking about, like,

00:04:56.562 --> 00:04:59.064
not being able to move
and how, you know,

00:04:59.064 --> 00:05:01.100
and not being able to do
things myself.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:02.502
And it got depressing, and then
the only way

00:05:02.502 --> 00:05:05.771
I really got myself out of it
was being, like,

00:05:05.771 --> 00:05:08.708
how much exciting things are
happening this year

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:11.377
and also how much, like,
By slowing things down

00:05:11.377 --> 00:05:13.612
and being appreciative
and being in the present moment

00:05:13.612 --> 00:05:15.514
and appreciating
the people around you

00:05:15.514 --> 00:05:18.117
can make you feel like
a million dollars.

00:05:18.117 --> 00:05:21.587
-Well, we love you, and I'm so
happy that you're okay.

00:05:21.587 --> 00:05:23.122
-Thank you.

00:05:23.122 --> 00:05:24.223
-Be careful.

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:25.324
I want you to come back
more and more.

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:26.392
We love you.

